import asm65816
import batscr_codes

/*
	IMPORTANT:		IF YOU ARE USING THE OLD "Animate_Enemy_Main", OR ANY "Animate_Enemy..." OR
					"Enemy_Float..." SCRIPTS, DELETE THEM ALL SINCE THIS SCRIPT AND "batscr_codes"
					REPLACES THEM. NO MORE STINKY AND CONFUSING CODE TO LOOK BACK!!!!!

	Main config file to edit the enemies and their animation settings.
	
	AnimateEnemy_Main:
	------------------
	This file is mostly a config file with some other ASM code below the AnimationTable that I
	combined it with this main file. The main difference from this and the old Animate_Eneny_Main
	file is not too much, except that each entry doesn't have a "speed" value. It is replaced
	with a "script" parameter, which is a label address so that the enemy can run that script.
	
	If you're familar with Catador's "movscr_codes", then it won't be too hard to use since I
	took inspiration from it. I also took inspiration from Cooprock's "Bowspr" script as well
	as I was developing the Bowspr Battle System, which also uses Action/Movement Scripts
	in battle.
	
	Special thanks to PhoenixBound for helping me with the original Animate_Enemy_Main and the 
	other Animate_Enemy and Enemy_Float files. With his assistance on those files, it made me
	realize how stinky and confusing my old orignal code was and how bad it was to even make
	new features for it.
	
	HOW TO USE:
	-----------
	
	* ANIMATION TABLE * 
	-------------------
	The Animation Table holds these data fields:
	* ID:	This matches with the enemy IDs in the enemy_config_table.yml file. You can safely
			ignore this and don't override this number with anything else. Nothing happens
			if you do override it however, but ideally you shouldn't. If Coilsnake ever supports
			more than 255 enemies, this should be able to extend beyond 1000s of enemies. Just
			add more entries to the table if you can.
	
	* Max Frames:	This states how much frames of your enemy sprite has. This doesn't have
					to be the max amount if your sprite doesn't use the full alloted canvas
					space.
					
	* Sprite Size:	This is the size of your sprite in each frame. The valid sizes are stated
					in the DEFINES section.
					
	* Canvas Size:	This is the size of your total canvas space. This also uses the same
					size values in the DEFINES section.
	
	* Script:	This parameter is basically a label to run the script from when the enemy
				is in battle. Without "batscr_codes", this whole file is practically useless.
*/

//+=+=+=+=+= DEFINES =+=+=+=+=+
define SIZE_32x32 = 1
define SIZE_64x32 = 2
define SIZE_32x64 = 3
define SIZE_64x64 = 4
define SIZE_128x64 = 5
define SIZE_128x128 = 6

command animated_sprite(id, max_frames, sprite_size, canvas_size, script_addr) {
	"[{byte max_frames} {byte sprite_size} {byte canvas_size} 00 {long script_addr}]"
}

AnimationTable: 
	animated_sprite(0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
	animated_sprite(1, 0, 0, 0, 0)
	animated_sprite(2, 0, 0, 0, 0)
	animated_sprite(3, 0, 0, 0, 0)
